<body>
<h1>Unit 24: Nuclear Physics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Modern Physics</p><p class="summary">Nuclear physics is where conservation laws become non-negotiable and where a handful of nucleons produce an enormous energy scale. The binding-energy curve is the centre of the unit: it explains why fusion and fission both release energy, and only in different regimes.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The nuclear radius scales as R = R0 A^(1/3) with R0 about 1.2 fm, which means nuclear density is roughly constant at about 2.3e17 kg/m^3.</li><li>Binding energy per nucleon peaks near iron-56 at about 8.8 MeV per nucleon, so fusion up to iron and fission beyond it both release energy.</li><li>Einstein's E = mc^2 converts the mass defect into binding energy; 1 u of mass corresponds to 931.5 MeV.</li><li>Radioactive decay is statistical: the half-life is a property of the nucleus, and no individual atom decides when it decays.</li><li>Neutron decay and nuclear reactions conserve charge, baryon number, energy, momentum and angular momentum, and every proposed reaction must satisfy all of them.</li><li>Doubly magic nuclei such as oxygen-16, with closed proton and neutron shells, show binding energies well above the smooth trend of their neighbours.</li><li>Binding energy per nucleon climbs to iron-56 and falls beyond it, which is the whole reason fusion powers stars and fission powers reactors: both release energy by moving matter towards the peak.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The semi-empirical mass formula is fitted to known nuclei, so its predictions are most reliable in the region where the coefficients were fitted and degrade for exotic nuclei.</li><li>Alpha decay proceeds by quantum tunnelling, and the extreme sensitivity of the half-life to the alpha energy and Q-value - the Geiger-Nuttall relation - is the essential experimental handle.</li><li>Fission and fusion release energy only because the products sit nearer the peak of the binding-energy curve than the fuel does.</li><li>Quasi-stable nuclei are predicted by the liquid-drop model, but shell effects require the nuclear shell model, and neither is exact.</li><li>The liquid-drop model gets nuclear masses roughly right but misses the shell and pairing effects that explain the magic numbers and the fine structure of fission barriers.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Energy equivalent of 1 u</td><td>931.5 MeV</td></tr><tr><td>Binding energy per nucleon at iron-56</td><td>about 8.8 MeV</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear radius constant R0</td><td>about 1.2 fm</td></tr><tr><td>Half-life of carbon-14</td><td>about 5730 years</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>What is the neutrino's mass?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>The US Department of Energy reported KATRIN's direct upper bound of 0.45 eV/c^2 in 2026; the exact value remains unknown.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How strong are the shell effects that make magic nuclei so stable?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Magic numbers are firmly established experimentally, but their quantitative origin in the nucleon-nucleon interaction remains a subtle many-body problem.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>What is the internal structure of the neutron?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>The neutron is not a point particle, and its charge and magnetic structure is being probed at accelerators and in precision atomic-physics experiments.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the binding energy per nucleon of a given isotope from the mass defect and place it relative to iron on the binding-energy curve.</li><li>Show that the activity of a radioactive sample falls exponentially, and estimate an age from the measured isotopic ratios.</li><li>Explain why the alpha-decay half-life is so sensitive to a small change in nuclear charge.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.energy.gov/science/np/articles/katrin-narrows-down-range-neutrinos-mass">US DOE: KATRIN narrows the range of the neutrino mass</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-1-properties-of-nuclei">OpenStax: Properties of Nuclei</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-2-nuclear-binding-energy">OpenStax: Nuclear Binding Energy</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay">OpenStax: Radioactive Decay</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
